## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006).

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Wait with cancellation - throws if timeout expires
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async overload
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken externalToken = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(externalToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return await task.WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (!externalToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` is long-running, it must cooperate

The fundamental shift: **cancellation is cooperative now**. If `work` doesn't check for cancellation, it keeps running even after the timeout fires. Modify the work delegate:

```csharp
// Before: no way to stop early (relied on Thread.Abort injecting an exception)
Func<int> work = () => {
    var sum = 0;
    foreach (var item in hugeCollection)
        sum += Process(item);
    return sum;
};

// After: check the token at regular intervals
Func<int> work = () => {
    var sum = 0;
    foreach (var item in hugeCollection)
    {
        cts.Token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        sum += Process(item);
    }
    return sum;
};
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| `work` never checks the token → runs forever after "timeout" | Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at loop checkpoints |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Always `using var cts = ...` to avoid timer leaks |
| Blocking I/O ignores cancellation (e.g., `socket.Receive`) | Switch to async overloads that accept `CancellationToken` |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` and swallowing it | Let it propagate; only catch at the orchestration boundary |
| Truly uncooperative code (3rd-party, native) | Move to a child **process** and use `Process.Kill()` — there is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` |

### What was removed

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Replacement |
|----|-----|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` / `cts.CancelAfter(timeout)` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested`, decide to continue |
| Manual `Thread` + `Join` | `Task.Run` + `Task.WaitAsync(token)` |

### Bottom Line

There is **no drop-in replacement** for `Thread.Abort`. The old code could kill any thread regardless of what it was doing. In .NET 8, the work must opt-in to cancellation. If you control the code, add token checks. If you don't, isolate it in a separate process.
